{"task":"transcribe","language":"english","duration":51.880001068115234,"text":"In 1471, Pope Sixtus IV did something nobody had done before. He gave the people of Rome a few ancient bronze statues and had them stand in a public building for everyone to see. Among them were the she-wolf feeding the twins, and a boy pulling a thorn out of his foot. That was the birth of the first public museum in the world, which officially opened its doors to visitors in 1734. Today, it fills two palaces facing each other across Michelangelo's Square, joined by an underground passage that runs beneath the paving. In the courtyard of the first palace, a head two and a half meters tall and a stone foot taller than you are wait for you, pieces of a statue of the Emperor Constantine that stood twelve meters high. In the second palace sits a marble river god, on whom Romans used to pin anonymous jokes about their rulers.","words":[{"word":"In","start":0,"end":0.5},{"word":"1471","start":0.5,"end":1.6799999475479126},{"word":"Pope","start":2.0999999046325684,"end":2.200000047683716},{"word":"Sixtus","start":2.200000047683716,"end":2.6600000858306885},{"word":"IV","start":2.6600000858306885,"end":3.140000104904175},{"word":"did","start":3.140000104904175,"end":3.5},{"word":"something","start":3.5,"end":3.859999895095825},{"word":"nobody","start":3.859999895095825,"end":4.380000114440918},{"word":"had","start":4.380000114440918,"end":4.639999866485596},{"word":"done","start":4.639999866485596,"end":4.820000171661377},{"word":"before","start":4.820000171661377,"end":5.199999809265137},{"word":"He","start":5.739999771118164,"end":5.920000076293945},{"word":"gave","start":5.920000076293945,"end":6.139999866485596},{"word":"the","start":6.139999866485596,"end":6.340000152587891},{"word":"people","start":6.340000152587891,"end":6.619999885559082},{"word":"of","start":6.619999885559082,"end":7.059999942779541},{"word":"Rome","start":7.059999942779541,"end":7.059999942779541},{"word":"a","start":7